  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 405 AM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThe dominance of the high pressure ridge to the north of SoFlo will \nfinally break down on Monday as guidance weakens the mid-lvl ridge \nacross the SE CONUS due to a developing trough/low complex. An \nassociated sfc cold front is shown gradually migrating southward and \nbecoming stationary along the northern portions of the peninsula. \n\nGlobal models show a potential low developing along the stalled \nfront around NE Florida, and slowly migrating northward across the \nwest Atlantic during the first half of the work week. The influence \nof the low will keep ongoing tropical moisture advection across the \nstate. NBM responds to this scenario by pushing highest afternoon \nPOPs into the 80-90% range each day through the end of the work \nweek. Coverage becomes widespread across SoFlo by Tuesday.  \n\nWith the weakening of the ridge to the north and the aforementioned \nlow gradually pushing northward, pressure gradients over the area \nwill weaken and bring winds across SoFlo to a light flow or even \ncalm at times during much of the work week. This will allow for \nafternoon sea breeze and storm outflow boundaries to drive storm \ndevelopment and motion. Main hazards will include damaging gusts, \nlightning strikes, and localized flooding. \n\nTemperatures seem to remain warm with afternoon highs in the upper \n80s to low 90s, along with heat index values in the low-mid \n100s.",
